"""
## Purpose and audience
End-to-end system tests for async workflows built from the promise, future,
channel, combinator, and work-stealing-queue primitives declared inline below
(feature #RT-020, category Runtime). These scenarios verify the components
work together, not in isolation. Audience: runtime engineers and reviewers of
the async stack.
## Operator workflow
Run: src/compiler_rust/target/bootstrap/simple run test/03_system/stdlib/async_promise_system_spec.spl
and read the SPEC FILE VERDICT line; every scenario must pass.
## Compatibility and limitations
The async primitives are exercised through the inline stub classes declared in
this file (no function-typed fields), so a failure here indicts the composed
workflows or the language semantics they lean on, not a shipped stdlib module
directly. All scenarios are deterministic — no real concurrency or timers.
## Verification guidance and troubleshooting
A failure names the composed workflow under test (creation/resolution,
promise-to-future flow, channels, combinators, error propagation, work
stealing, chaining, close semantics, full pipeline). Re-run after touching the
inline Promise/SimpleFuture/Channel/WorkStealingQueue declarations; a chain
failure usually means value extraction through the state enum drifted.
"""
